/*  stopPreT -- stop() WORKS WITH A REFUSAL STANDING. Tony's ruling, 2026-09-17:
    A REFUSAL'S SCOPE IS THE STATEMENT, and stop() works as intended, always.

    ⚠ THIS FILE WAS BUILT THE DAY BEFORE TO PIN THE OPPOSITE, AND SAYS SO RATHER THAN
    BEING QUIETLY REWRITTEN. SP-2 used to be a red-shaped pin -- a statement BELOW stop()
    that ran, because an outstanding refusal silenced command dispatch and stop() was
    never entered. The statement boundary retired that behaviour, so SP-2 flips to
    asserting its own ABSENCE, paired with the positive fact that makes the absence
    readable: `stop: end parsing`. docs/refusalScope.md carries the argument.

    THE ROWS
      SP-1  a plain statement after the refusal still runs -- a refusal is not a halt,
            it is scoped to its statement. ⚠ This is also SP-2's ANTI-VACUITY SIBLING:
            without it, SP-2's absence could be a file that simply died early.
      SP-2  the statement below stop() does NOT run  -- pinned as a COUNT of 0
      SP-3  `stop: end parsing` -- THE POSITIVE FACT. An absence alone asserts nothing;
            this is what says the stop actually fired rather than the file ending.
      SP-4  the refusal itself, by message -- without it the whole fixture is vacuous,
            because a run with no refusal would pass every row above trivially.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;
register(StopPreT);
define
    spRoot;
    ;
spX := spRoot.*spNope;
cerr "SP-1 a plain statement after the refusal STILL RAN":;
stop();
cerr "SP-2 a statement BELOW stop() RAN -- stop() was never entered":;
